ncial reasons, the couple decides that they are going to stop trying when they have three children, whether they have a boy or not. (We are assuming that having a boy or a girl is equally likely, and that the child’s gender in each birth is independent of the gender in the other births.)","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"key":"tR6GQvqoJs"}],"key":"jq7wDQ1DmW"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let the random variable X be the number of children the couple has.","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"key":"ajysvUGcCQ"}],"key":"eWRmCJ1Ydy"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Our goal is to find the probability distribution of X. In other words, we would like to create a table that lists all the possible values of X and the corresponding probabilities. We’ll follow the same steps we followed in the two examples we solved.","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"key":"IwJ7IQQd9M"}],"key":"iefRNWJ5Wg"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-ab552a073177c801fc08fc2e7e8860b3.mjs","model":{"question":"What are the possible outcomes of this \"experiment,\" and their probabilities?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"B (1/2), GB (1/4), GGB (1/8), GGG (1/8)","feedback":"Correct! P(B) = 1/2; P(GB) = $(1/2)(1/2) = 1/4$; P(GGB) = 1/8; P(GGG) = 1/8."},{"correct":false,"text":"B, BG, BB, GG — each with probability 1/4","feedback":"The couple stops after a boy, so sequences like BG or BB can't happen."},{"correct":false,"text":"B, GB, GGB, GGG — each with probability 1/4","feedback":"The four outcomes are not equally likely—stopping early makes B much more likely than GGB."}],"multi":false,"hint":"The couple stops at the first boy, or after three children. The possible sequences are B, GB, GGB, GGG.","explanation":""},"key":"d5oltC1KpM"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-ab552a073177c801fc08fc2e7e8860b3.mjs","model":{"question":"What is P(X = 1), the probability that the couple has exactly one child?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"1/2","feedback":"Correct! $X = 1$ only for the outcome B, so P(X = $1) = 1/2$."},{"correct":false,"text":"1/4","feedback":"1/4 is the probability of GB, which gives two children."},{"correct":false,"text":"1/8","feedback":"With four possible outcomes but unequal probabilities, don't use 1/4 per outcome."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Which outcome gives exactly one child?","explanation":""},"key":"F5M4xpypN4"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-ab552a073177c801fc08fc2e7e8860b3.mjs","model":{"question":"What is P(X = 3), the probability that the couple has three children?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"1/4","feedback":"Correct! P(X = 3) = P(GGB) + P(GGG) = $1/8 + 1/8 = 1/4$."},{"correct":false,"text":"1/8","feedback":"1/8 counts only one of the two outcomes with three children."},{"correct":false,"text":"1/2","feedback":"Check: the probability of two girls first is 1/4, and then the third child's gender doesn't change the count of children."}],"multi":false,"hint":"$X = 3$ happens for GGB or GGG.","explanation":""},"key":"Ov0cTJDhBS"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":76,"column":1},"end":{"line":76,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"So the probability distribution of X is:","position":{"start":{"line":76,"column":1},"end":{"line":76,"column":1}},"key":"R0LtnUf70R"}],"key":"aWzFzqjqaL"},{"type":"table","position":{"start":{"line":78,"column":1},"end":{"line":80,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableRow","position":{"start":{"line":78,"column":1},"end":{"line":78,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableCell","header":true,"position":{"start":{"line":78,"column":1},"end":{"line":78,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"x","posi